scopeMarkdown:
**macOS only.** This epic must be executed on a real Mac (Apple Silicon or Intel) with Xcode installed, so that `xcrun metal`, the iOS SDK and a Metal device are available. Nothing here can be closed from Windows or Linux.

It turns the four handoff notes in `ToFix/` (written 2026-10-06 after the macOS CI failure in run 37500248162) into verified, publishable work:

1. `ToFix/01-macos-metal-renderer.md`: the Metal renderer port to the premultiplied `PaintColor` paint contract (branch `fix/metal-paint-contract`, commits `3ad5fca` and `5321a25`). Real macOS CI already compiled its shaders; it still needs macOS tests and runtime proof.
2. `ToFix/02-macos-zed-util-process-reaping.md`: `gpux-zed-util` does not compile on macOS. `crates/util/src/command/darwin.rs` calls `Child::adopt_raw_pid`, which exists only in Zed's git fork of `async-process`. This is the next CI blocker. The note embeds a proposed, type-checked but never-run replacement.
3. `ToFix/03-wgpu-dashed-borders.md`: the WGPU dashed-border fix (branch `fix/wgpu-dashed-border-gaps`, `c6b6512`/`e7149e7`) and its paired RayX Test Suite spec (RayX branch `test/dashed-border-gaps`, `f1a9be7b`). Both are done and runtime-proven on Windows; they still need to land.
4. `ToFix/04-flaky-windows-process-test.md`: the upstream Windows test `test_drop_terminates_grandchildren` timed out once in CI. Verified during planning: the re-run of that job passed, and upstream Zed `main` still uses the same 5 second deadline (last change `c642b422de`).

In scope:
- making CI's macOS step (`cargo check --workspace --all-targets`) pass;
- proving the Metal paint contract with automated headless Metal pixel tests and a native RayX Test Suite run;
- carrying the dashed-border fix and proving it on the Mac;
- hardening the flaky Windows test;
- recording the durable knowledge;
- publishing one pull request with all CI jobs green.

Out of scope:
- merging to `main` (the user decides after review);
- any change to the `gpux-wgpu` fork in `vendor/wgpu`;
- Metal shader effects (they stay capability-false);
- iOS device or simulator runs (only the iOS compile is checked);
- new features;
- RayX Lab.

Unresolved product decisions, with recorded defaults:
- **One pull request or several.** Default: one Gpux pull request from a single integration branch plus the paired RayX pull request. The user may ask to split them.
- **Whether and when to merge after green CI.** The user decides.
- **Hardening the Windows test or leaving it.** Default: harden it with a longer deadline, recorded as a gpux patch.

constraintsMarkdown:
- **One working branch per repository.** In Gpux, work on a single integration branch `macos-only/metal-port-and-ci`, created from `fix/metal-paint-contract`. In RayX, work on `test/dashed-border-gaps`. This epic directory and `ToFix/` were left uncommitted by the planning session; commit them to the integration branch first so they travel with the work. RayX builds against whatever branch the sibling `../Gpux` checkout has.
- **Dependencies come from crates.io only.** No git dependencies and no `[patch]` sections (gpux `CLAUDE.md`, packaging rules). The `async-process` fork used upstream is therefore unavailable.
- **Keep the vendored wgpu fork untouched.** Do not modify `vendor/wgpu`; the fork carries exactly one change.
- **Keep crate boundaries and upstream reviewability.** Keep Zed's crate boundaries. Prefer gpux-owned additions in new files over large rewrites of imported upstream files, and record every replay rule in `.claude/skills/gpux/references/update-from-zed-gpui-closure.md`.
- **Preserve packed Metal buffer ABIs.** Convert cbindgen-exported array fields to Metal vector types at their use sites in shader helpers (gpux skill rule).
- **Runtime proof goes through RayX's Test Suite.** Use `../RayX`, `apps/test_suite`; never use `apps/lab` and never add fixtures there.
- **Test only what changed.** The macOS workspace check is CI's own command, and the reason it is allowed is recorded on that check. Raw logs and screenshots go to ignored `artifacts-temp/agentx/macos-only-metal-port-and-ci-repair/`, never into `.agentx/` or commits.
- **Commits.** Use Conventional Commits with meaningful scopes and no Claude co-author lines.
- **Publication needs explicit approval.** Push, PR creation and merge require the user's explicit approval at the time they happen; this epic never merges.
- **Environment prerequisites on the Mac:**
  - Xcode (`xcrun metal`, iOS SDK);
  - the checked-in Rust toolchain with `rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios`;
  - nightly Rust for xtask's `-Z build-std`, and `wasm-bindgen-cli` 0.2.129;
  - Node 20+, pnpm 10+ and Playwright Chromium in `../RayX/tools-node`;
  - the RayX sibling repositories `RayX.Dtcg`, `RayX.Reactive` and `RayX.SignalR` checked out beside RayX;
  - an authenticated `gh` CLI.

requirements.descriptionMarkdown:
`crates/util/src/command/darwin.rs` (imported from Zed `c24358d`) spawns children with `posix_spawnp`. It keeps behavior the rest of gpux relies on:
- `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT` plus `FD_CLOEXEC` pipes, so a piped git child cannot deadlock on fds leaked into concurrent spawns;
- reset exception ports;
- `SIGPIPE` reset to `SIG_DFL`;
- `chdir` through file actions;
- PATH resolution against the child's environment.

It then calls `smol::process::Child::adopt_raw_pid(pid, true, kill_on_drop)`, an API that only Zed's git fork of `async-process` provides (upstream `Cargo.toml` `[patch.crates-io]`, rev `0b6d6713570af61806e1e5cb40e0f757cb93fd9d`). crates.io `async-process` 2.5.0 lacks it, so the crate has never compiled on macOS in gpux.

CI's `check (macos-latest)` job runs `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`; it has never passed. After PR #1 the Metal shaders compile on real macOS (run 37520997553), and the job stopped at `gpux-zed-util` (REQ-PROC). The Windows cross-checks could not reach the crates that need a C toolchain:
- `gpux-zed-util`;
- `gpux-bench-metrics`;
- `gpux-http-client-tls`;
- `gpux-reqwest-client`;
- the dev and bench targets of `gpux` and `gpux-platform`.

More macOS-only errors may be waiting there. `gpux-apple` is shared with iOS and has a `runtime_shaders` feature that stitches `scene.h` into `shaders.metal` for runtime compilation.

Branch `fix/metal-paint-contract` ports the Metal renderer to the `PaintColor` contract that WGPU adopted in cb4130b and d5823fe: premultiplied, gamma-encoded sRGB paint in and out, composited in encoded sRGB like Figma and CSS. The port includes:
- cbindgen parses `gpux_color`'s `paint.rs`;
- `MonochromeSpriteKind` becomes `#[repr(C)]`;
- the shaders consume `PaintColor`;
- premultiplied blending in the general pipelines;
- premultiplied OKLab stops;
- dashed borders fade the whole color;
- inset shadows.

None of it has run on a Mac yet. `ToFix/01-macos-metal-renderer.md` lists the expected results and the hardware double-checks.
